[
  {
    "file": "crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs",
    "line": 82,
    "summary": "`part` treats every named part that is not `Pick` as a base, so a local object-literal alias the props type is built from (`type Props = Base & OwnProps; type OwnProps = { variant?: ... }`) never adds its fields to `own`.",
    "failure_scenario": "`type OwnProps = { variant?: 'a' | 'b' }; type Props = Base & OwnProps`. The structural lane lists `variant` with `parent` None and it is in no layer, so `own=false`, `overrides=false`, `based=false`. If the body is complete and never reads `variant`, `decide` puts this hand-written prop in `collapsed` instead of `rows`. The same happens through `Passthrough` (`type Props = OwnProps`)."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs",
    "line": 67,
    "summary": "`read` ignores the alias variants `Partial`, `Required`, `Union` and `Omit` (the `_ => {}` arm), so their own fields and picked keys are lost.",
    "failure_scenario": "`type Props = Partial<{ size: string }>`, or a discriminated union `type Props = { kind: 'a'; a: string } | { kind: 'b'; b: string }`, or `type Props = Omit<Base,'x'> & ...` written as an `Omit` alias. In each case `own` and `picked` come out empty, so every member looks inherited and can be collapsed when the body does not read it."
  },
  {
    "file": "crates/core/src/relevance/props_type.rs",
    "line": 49,
    "summary": "Names inside the props type (`extends` bases, `Pick` key aliases, intersection parts) are looked up with the component file's `lookup`, not the declaring file. The alias's own `file_path` is ignored, which `CollectedTypeAlias::file_path`'s doc warns against.",
    "failure_scenario": "`Button.tsx` imports `ButtonProps` from `types.ts`, where `type Keys = 'a' | 'b'; interface ButtonProps extends Pick<Base, Keys>`. `lookup(\"Keys\")` runs in `Button.tsx`, finds nothing (or a different local `Keys`), so `picked` is empty or wrong."
  },
